<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu (state)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">to 473 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu was a state of the Zhou period in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> region, centred on modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, whose rulers claimed descent from Taibo, an uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a>. It rose to power in the late sixth century BCE under King Helü, who defeated Chu at Boju in 506 BCE, and under his son Fuchai, who challenged Jin for leadership of the states. It was conquered by its southern neighbour Yue under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">Goujian</a> in 473 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-state.jpg" alt="Wu (state)" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yug · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_plain_5c._BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
